/Type /StructElem
/S /H4
/P 32 0 R
/T (The Location Quotient \(LQ\) is a ratio that is used to identify the significance of employment of an industry sector in a local economy compared to a larger reference economy.)
/E (The Location Quotient \(LQ\) is a ratio that is used to identify the significance of employment of an industry sector in a local economy compared to a larger reference economy.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [19]
>>
endobj
259 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H4
/P 32 0 R
/T (An LQ below 1 indicates that an industry under represented in the local economy compared to the reference economy. An LQ of 1 suggests that the industry in both the local and reference economy are at equilibrium in their respective economies. When the LQ is greater than 1, it indicates that the industry more concentrated locally than the reference economy. Moreover, an LQ of greater than 1.25 typically indicates that the industry is an export-oriented based, contributing to economic growth that will further strengthen the local economy \(Leigh & Blakely, 2016\).)
/E (An LQ below 1 indicates that an industry under represented in the local economy compared to the reference economy. An LQ of 1 suggests that the industry in both the local and reference economy are at equilibrium in their respective economies. When the LQ is greater than 1, it indicates that the industry more concentrated locally than the reference economy. Moreover, an LQ of greater than 1.25 typically indicates that the industry is an export-oriented based, contributing to economic growth that will further strengthen the local economy \(Leigh & Blakely, 2016\).)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [20]
>>
endobj
